Z||000000||fierospeeder||11-22-2000||03:29 PM||||I read the auto times section in the paper and it states that GM and other car manufacturers are putting in a new idiot light in vehicles for 2002?  Its a sensor that is someplace on the oil pan, and it will sense the amount of "conductivity" forgot what it was, of oil.  As oil gets worn out, the sensor will be able to pick that up and tell the "user" i mean driver that they should change the oil.  <P>Expected oil changes are going to be 7,000-10,000 miles.  GM has studied the wear on oil on a million mile study.<P>My oil gets dirty after 2,000 miles eventhough my car doesn't lose any oil.  So are the engine tolerances so much closer and carbon is almost eliminated because of the cleaner burning engines?  Anyone like this idea or is it another sensor to change after we buy one of these cars 15 years from now when they only cost a few thousand, like most of the cars i buy now.||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||11-22-2000||04:24 PM||||Just another idiot light that people will ignore.<P>Really a good idea in that the principle behind it is valid, and oils continue to improve.<P>Cleaner engines are another factor. As to replacing the sensor, sure...it will probably wear out and require oil pan removal or some such process.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-22-2000||07:00 PM||||I expect GM is more likely to just program the PCM to start _____ing after so many miles. It's much cheaper than adding a sensor. Either way I expect the light to quickly get ripped out of allot of cars.<P>I remember seeing this on some kind of car years ago. was some thing built into the dash that you were suposed to reset at oil changes. It didn't make people change the oil more either. They just ignored the thing. Most shops could never find the fragging reset anyway or just didn't bother if they did know where it was.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||11-22-2000||07:45 PM||||theogre,<BR>they said something about that also, doing it without the sensor.<P>They will program the ecm or computer so that it will observe the kind of driving is done to the car.  If it is short distances, highway driving, high rpms etc....<P>If they use the sensor, its suppose also tell if your oil level is low,  my 87 tbird turbo has a idiot light for low oil level.<BR>the problem is after age, the seal gets worn out, and the outside of the sensor gets moist with oil or may leak, i put rtv where the wire comes out.<P><BR>I said this before, about the story of a guy going to an oil change with a brand new SUV explorer.  After leaving, i think it was his oil filter (or drain bolt) got knocked loose and he lost all of his oil. Eventually his motor got locked up.  If he looked at his oil pressure light or gauge, he probably wouldn't be sueing the oil change place for 4,000 bucks for a new engine, ford had to put in.  &lt;tear&gt;<BR>||||reg||1
